Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final  Type  Surgical Pathology  Pathology Report  ML 11  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   CASE  ML 11  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME   Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  MGH Outpatient    Consulting Pathologist s   None  Assigned Resident Path  Miguel N Rivera  M D   Clinical History  Thrombocytosis    Reports To   GEORGE VELMAHOS  M D         FINAL DIAGNOSIS     TEST PERFORMED    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_CASE_ID G T   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Mutation Analysis    INDICATION FOR TEST     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  mutation    RESULTS    NEGATIVE  no  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  c  REMOVED_CASE_ID G T   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   mutant allele extension product peak was  detected when testing DNA from the patient s blood specimen  The wild type  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   c  REMOVED_CASE_ID G extension product peak was detected     INTERPRETATION   Published reports indicate the presence of the  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  mutation is evidence  supporting a diagnosis of a chronic myeloproliferative disorder  namely  Polycythemia Vera  Essential Thrombocythemia or Idiopathic Myelofibrosis     COMMENT  A low tumor burden  less than 2 in 100 cells with heterozygous  mutation in the peripheral blood  may result in a false negative result     TEST INFORMATION   A recurrent point mutation in the  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  kinase  exon 14  c  REMOVED_CASE_ID G T   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    has  been found in a majority of Polycythemia Vera cases  and a subset of Essential  Thrombocythemia and Idiopathic Myelofibrosis  Agnogenic Myeloid Metaplasia   cases  The identification of this mutation has diagnostic utility and has not  been found in normal or reactive conditions  It is rarely found in other  myeloproliferative disorders  We have developed a single base extension assay   SNaPshot  to genotype c  REMOVED_CASE_ID  in the forward and reverse directions from  genomic DNA samples from blood  bone marrow or splenectomies with  extramedullary hematopoiesis specimens  The analytic sensitivity of detecting  the mutation has been established in our laboratory at approximately 1  mutant  allele frequency or 2  heterozygous mutant cells     Baxter  E  J   L  M  Scott  et al    REMOVED_CASE_ID     Acquired mutation of the tyrosine  kinase  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  in human myeloproliferative disorders   Lancet 365  REMOVED_CASE_ID    REMOVED_DATE  James  C   V  Ugo  et al    REMOVED_CASE_ID     A unique clonal  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  mutation leading to  constitutive signaling causes polycythaemia vera   Nature 434  REMOVED_CASE_ID    REMOVED_DATE  Kralovics  R   F  Passamonti  et al    REMOVED_CASE_ID     A gain of function mutation of   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  in myeloproliferative disorders   N Engl J Med 352 17   REMOVED_DATE  Levine  R  L   M  Wadleigh  et al    REMOVED_CASE_ID     Activating mutation in the tyrosine  kinase  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  in polycythemia vera  essential thrombocythemia  and myeloid  metaplasia with myelofibrosis   Cancer Cell 7 4   REMOVED_DATE  Zhao  R   S  Xing  et al    REMOVED_CASE_ID     Identification of an acquired  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  mutation  in polycythemia vera   J Biol Chem 280 24   REMOVED_DATE    This test was developed  and its performance characteristics were determined by  the MGH Diagnostic Molecular Pathology Laboratory  It has not been cleared or  approved by the U S  Food and Drug Administration  The FDA has determined that  such clearance or approval is not necessary  This test is used for clinical  purposes  Pursuant to the requirements of CLIA  88  this laboratory has  established and verified the test s accuracy and precision     Questions concerning these values should be addressed to Dr  A  John Iafrate at   617  REMOVED_DATE MGH internal address  Diagnostic Molecular Pathology  Laboratory  Massachusetts General Hospital  55 Fruit St   Warren 508  Boston   MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID          Final Diagnosis by Miguel N Rivera M D