Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Updated  Type  Surgical Pathology  Pathology Report  MS 10  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   CASE  MS 10  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME   Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  MGH Inpatient    Path Subspecialty Service  GI  Small  Results To  CARLO G TRAVERSO  MBBCH  PH   Signed Out by  Jochen Lennerz  M D   Ph D              Addended Report                Reason for Addendum  1  Additional Diagnostic Information  CLINICAL DATA   Patient with lower GIB  colonoscopy screening for ascending circumferential  inflammatory mass  rule out IBD versus malignancy  Patient also has liver and  lung nodules by CT scan       FINAL DIAGNOSIS   COLON  ASCENDING MASS   BIOPSY   POORLY DIFFERENTIATED INVASIVE ADENOCARCINOMA   SEE NOTE      NOTE  Sections show a mixture of invasive glands  small abortive epithelial  structures as well as isolated invasive epithelial cells  The malignant glands  are cribriform and show focal mucin production  The findings are consistent  with a colorectal carcinoma  However  the lesion is predominantly located in  the deeper aspects of the sample and the overlying colonic mucosa shows no  adenomatous changes  Given a  the presentation with nodular lesions in liver  and lungs  b  the absence of surrounding dysplasia and c  the histologic  appearance somewhat unusual for conventional colorectal carcinoma  we would  like to broaden the morphologic differential diagnosis to include a  gastrointestinal  i e  appendiceal  gastric  pancreaticobiliary   genitourinary  or breast primary  Additional immunohistochemistry is pending and results will  be issued in an addendum     Findings have been discussed with Dr  Traverso on REMOVED_MONTH 21   REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 9 am     COLON  ASCENDING   BIOPSY   EDEMATOUS COLONIC MUCOSA   THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY     COLON  TRANSVERSE   BIOPSY   COLONIC MUCOSA WITH NO DIAGNOSTIC ABNORMALITY     COLON  DESCENDING   BIOPSY   COLONIC MUCOSA WITH NO DIAGNOSTIC ABNORMALITY       GROSS DESCRIPTION   Received are 4 containers which are  labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME   Johnson  M D    REMOVE_MRN     Received in formalin  labeled  ascending mass   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 2cm  aggregate of tan pink irregular soft tissues submitted in toto as cassette  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID      Received in formalin  labeled  ascending colon   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 2cm  aggregate of tan pink irregular soft tissues submitted in toto as cassette  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID      Received in formalin  labeled  transverse   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 2cm aggregate  of tan pink irregular soft tissues submitted in toto as cassette  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID      Received in formalin  labeled  descending   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 2cm aggregate  of tan pink irregular soft tissues submitted in toto as cassette  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID                Final Diagnosis by Jochen Lennerz M D   Ph D   Electronically signed on  Wednesday REMOVED_MONTH 21   REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 09 43 13AM  By his her signature above  the pathologist listed as making the Final  Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed  or corrected the diagnoses               ADDENDUM NOTE   REASON  Additional Diagnostic Information    The retained material of the patient s original breast carcinoma  MS 82  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    was sectioned  6 blocks  and morphologic comparison of the ductal carcinoma  with the patient s current carcinoma in the colon is NOT consistent with a  metastatic lesion  In addition  ER  PR  and GCDFP 15 immunohistochemistry is  negative in the colonic carcinoma  Additional immunophenotypic  characterization of the current carcinoma shows very focal positivity for the  intestinal transcription factor  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   very focal immunoreactivity for  cytokeratin 20  and no immunoreactivity with cytokeratin 7  All three markers   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  highlight the overlying and adjacent normal colonic  epithelium     Taken together  the findings are consistent with a colorectal primary  and  given the variant morphologic features  focal mucinous differentiation and  absence of adenomatous lesion  hereditary non polypous HNPCC  Lynch syndrome   should be considered  We recommend microsatellite instability testing and  screening for a mismatch repair defect  In this particular setting  screening  by immunohistochemistry for the expression of mismatch repair genes is  considered a genetic test and should be part of genetic counseling with  informed consent     Addendum  1 by Jochen Lennerz M D   Ph D   Electronically signed on Tuesday  REMOVED_MONTH 27   REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 04 54 56PM  By his her signature above  the pathologist listed as making the Final  Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed  or corrected the diagnoses     PART A  Colon  Ascending Mass   Biopsy  PART B  Colon  Ascending   Biopsy  PART C  Colon  Transverse   Biopsy  PART D  Colon  Descending   Biopsy       report_end  , Positive.
