Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Updated  Type  Surgical Pathology  Pathology Report  MS 10  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   CASE  MS 10  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME   Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Neuropathology Center  CRP 8    Path Subspecialty Service  Neuropath  Results To  USHARBUDH SOHUR  M D   NAMITA GOYAL  M D   Resident  Christian J  Davidson  M D   Signed Out by  E  Tessa Hedley Whyte  M D              Addended Report                Reason for Addendum  1  Additional Electron Microscopy Results  CLINICAL DATA   The patient is a 34 year old female being evaluated for a mitochondrial  disease  Her symptoms began in her teenage years when she noted increased  susceptibility for infections  colds   viral illnesses and was affected by  chronic fatigue  She has had joint pains  ankles  low back  shoulders  since  approximately  REMOVED_CASE_ID  and has been undergoing evaluations treatment for possible  inflammatory arthritis   In the past year  she has noted various symptoms  including chronic fatigue  lightheadedness  diagnosed with postural  hypotension   reflux  diarrhea constipation  chest pain on exertion  episodes  of shortness of breath  and episodes of generalized body weakness with muscle  aches  Her work up thus far has included a normal CK level of 123   Her  cardiopulmonary level III testing revealed a depressed  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  at max exercise and  raised the possibility of an oxidative myopathy   Thus  a muscle biopsy is  requested to evaluate for mitochondrial cytopathy         FINAL DIAGNOSIS   SKELETAL MUSCLE  LEFT QUADRICEPS      MUSCLE WITH RARE SUBSARCOLEMMAL ACCUMULATION OF MITOCHONDRIA AND ONE  COX NEGATIVE SDH  POSITIVE FIBER  SEE NOTE      NOTE  Microscopic examination shows a type 2 fiber predominant muscle with  normally distributed fiber sizes  There is no evidence of inflammation   degeneration  myonecrosis  or increased number of internalized nuclei  Gomori  trichome shows rare fibers with subsarcolemmal red stain  but shows a  predominantly normal pattern of staining with no evidence of ragged red fibers   nemaline rods  inclusions or increased interstitial fibrosis  PAS and oil red  O stains show normal patterns of staining of all fibers with no evidence of  glycogen or lipid accumulation   NADH staining is unremarkable and shows no  moth eaten fibers  ATPase at pH REMOVED_DATE  REMOVED_DATE  and REMOVED_DATE reveal a type 2 fiber  predominant muscle     COX stain shows one COX negative fiber  SDH stain shows no negative fibers and  highlights rare fibers with subsarcolemmal accumulation of mitochondria     The diagnosis is based on the examination of hematoxylin and eosin stained  slides  modified Gomori s trichrome  NADH reductase  oil red O  PAS and ATPase   at pH REMOVED_DATE  REMOVED_DATE  and 9 4  stains     Results of one micron epon and electron microscopic examination will be  reported in an addendum       GROSS DESCRIPTION   Received fresh are three pieces of soft red muscle measuring 2 0cm x 1 0cm x  0 5cm in aggregate  The tissue is divided for enzyme histochemistry  EM  studies and remaining tissue is frozen in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  for prospective studies           Final Diagnosis by E  Tessa Hedley Whyte M D   Electronically signed on  Monday REMOVED_MONTH 29   REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 04 27 28PM  By his her signature above  the pathologist listed as making the Final  Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed  or corrected the diagnoses           ADDENDUM NOTE   REASON  One Micron Epon and Electron Microscopy Results    The one micron section reveals mild variation in fiber size  Electron  microscopy shows scattered fibers with accumulation of mitochondria beneath the  sarcolemmal membrane  The mitochondria are predominantly normal in size and  internal architecture  with rare mitochondria with complex cristae  While  there is mainly normal architecture of the contractile apparatus  one small  fiber shows myofibril disarray  There are scattered myelin figures  No  glycogen or lipid accumulation is noted  There is focally thickened vascular  basement membrane               Addendum  1 by E  Tessa Hedley Whyte M D   Electronically signed on Thursday  REMOVED_MONTH 30   REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 04 13 44PM  By his her signature above  the pathologist listed as making the Final  Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed  or corrected the diagnoses     PART   Muscle  left quadriceps   Biopsy       report_end  , Positive.
