Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Corrected Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  ACCESSIONED ON  02 MAY 95 at 12 49 CLINICAL DATA   None Provided      REVISED REPORT     Reason for Revision  ADDITIONAL TISSUE RECEIVED Revised by  Dennis Sgroi  M D  Revised on 05 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 13 50                                                                                     FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   95 854   1  INFILTRATING DUCTAL CARCINOMA  98    REMOVED_DATE CM  POORLY DIFFERENTIATED  GRADE III III   PRESENT AT A TISSUE SURFACE  AND WITH EXTENSIVE NECROSIS   SEE NOTE   2  INTRADUCTAL CARCINOMA  2    COMEDO TYPE  Note  Definite blood vascular and lymphatic invasion is not seen  Diagnosis by  Dennis Sgroi  M D  Signed On  15 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 08 39                                                                                     IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  LEFT   THE TUMOR CELLS DO NOT STAIN FOR ESTROGEN RECEPTOR PROTEIN  THE TUMOR CELLS DO NOT STAIN FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN  SEE NOTE  NOTE  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY  THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES  Added by  Dennis Sgroi  M D  Signed On  15 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 08 39                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH OUTSIDE LOCATION REPORTS TO  Michele Annette Gadd  M D  James F Mcintyre  M D  Michael L Grossbard  M D  PART   LEFT BREAST  95 854  Date of Procedure  17 APR  REMOVED_CASE_ID  Slides  1 Blocks  1   report_end  , Negative.
